045417] [17592186433376 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433376 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433376 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433376 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433376 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433376 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433376 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186433367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433376 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8390ddee-9042-5713-b922-461ba822e40f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433377 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433377 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433377 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433377 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/76/76cbc53a671cb7164352b2c71931379a6dab936a966ded05dc93ba31da013dfe.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global minimum of the function?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433377 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "05d8ae1c-da42-5a43-aa56-47e108152b59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433377 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-4.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-4.$ The function reaches this value at both $(x,y)=(-3,-6)$ and $(x,y)=(-3,6).$\n\n![](images/78/78348020535973cddc75c2d935c0ed2f0390b5330b4139105be80cb8f6e6032a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433378 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433378 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163794" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433378 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433378 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f8/f82404522255a2e5b8be8b45d39062bd2239c15294024c02d6d4ad3dd3f44809.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global minimum points of the function?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433378 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ccb6a2f-baf3-5789-838a-0e294ce49920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433378 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-2.5.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-2.5.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-1,2),$ so the global minimum point is $(-1,2,-2.5).$\n\n![](images/29/29d2b703b9c17aa69bb41076e4bf40f509af4b44bd3ade14dbdeaec7cad25120.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433379 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433379 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163795" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433379 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433379 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a3/a39696fcb202485b9a58839609ad807e43397fde8df965bed2824f6cdc307703.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433379 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e0f0650-f17e-5833-8166-da3fd9367f23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433379 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=1.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $1.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-1,-2)$ and  $(x,y)=(1,0),$so the global maximum points are $(-1,-2,1)$ and $(1,0,1).$\n\n![](images/4a/4a818d3e3e3c6c6a90ff9e69d808803016114894fbc605de5d21c75817eb8cf3.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434665 #edb/ref 1080 "$A$ and $B$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434665 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434665 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2683d2d1-d298-565b-a7c7-9a4e0ec1b79e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434666 #edb/ref 1080 "local minimum" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434666 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434666 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "270cb157-312b-598a-a963-8332f3594021" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434668 #edb/ref 1080 "$B$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434668 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434668 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3674a427-b1f6-5423-9cad-a53248cc1526" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434669 #edb/ref 1080 "$C$ and $D$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434669 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434669 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3af27a0e-03ce-5737-81df-4b4e8b4ce792" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434670 #edb/ref 1080 "$(1,2,2)$ and $(-1,-2,1)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434670 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434670 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40d51ffd-3c87-5270-834d-5cb8714970fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434676 #edb/ref 1080 "local maximum" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434676 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434676 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "48aa8c46-39c6-5468-85c5-ec68e86acbdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434679 #edb/ref 1080 "inconclusive" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434679 #ed