es not have local maxima" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434727 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434727 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ecbd6041-7dad-5acb-839a-6742c3614749" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434728 #edb/ref 1080 "saddle point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434728 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434728 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ee820c78-33c5-521b-97ae-0bef262419cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434735 #edb/ref 1080 "$(2,3,0)$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434735 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434735 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fd7d1370-1b00-5b14-93b1-c569a931408e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434737 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434737 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434737 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434737 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434737 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434737 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434737 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434737 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434737 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2bbf6504-629f-56a6-8d02-ea8b2cdc4409" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434741 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434741 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434741 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434741 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6988e8b1-324d-5035-b234-84ec660debef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434742 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434742 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434742 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434742 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434742 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434742 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434742 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434742 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434742 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "812379f4-7ca2-54a9-9137-3e6202ac33a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434747 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "caaf0180-b775-51a5-8357-5dbad212c145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434752 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434752 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434752 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434752 #edb/ref 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1+\\sqrt{5},$ what type of point is $a?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186434752 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "463ee037-d37e-5536-bfb1-df9c529010e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434752 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both negative and positive eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =1-\\sqrt{5}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =1+\\sqrt{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434753 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434753 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163772" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434753 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434753 #edb/ref 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ what type of point is $a?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186434753 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c7c764b-5efc-585f-b1d6-ab32ef1c26ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434753 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =1>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434754 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475156 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434754 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475157 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434754 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475158 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434754 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434754 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434754 #edb/ref 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈5+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=8$\n\nII. $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local maximum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186434754 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ec0f059-837e-5e3d-9bb4-8d292f2af4e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434754 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-3$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|=8.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-3<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|=8>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434756 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434756 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164151" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434756 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434756 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f7/f7429a2fc291fc9e14685208288f670cca341a260ac13b358c52d99c35e13b4f.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434756 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73e92678-31f3-525b-9096-a06391739cab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434756 #edb/ref 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $A$ and $D.$ Hence, $A$ and $D$ are local minima.\n\n![](images/b8/b8952aa59c9351c9723cd62f35073d46d7ddfd46afa9a9b3ddae7ce149065c59.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434759 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475153 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434759 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475154 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434759 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475155 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434759 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163778" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434759 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434759 #edb/ref 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=1$ and $det(A)=4$\n\nII. $A$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local maximum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186434759 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6771fc8-6834-5ff6-940e-46f1464b61f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434759 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=1$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=1>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434764 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475162 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434764 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475163 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434764 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475164 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434764 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434764 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434764 #edb/ref 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)=-15$\n\nII. $A$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local minimum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186434764 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5518b1c-8ee2-55b4-900e-3e5d5d3b8c0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434764 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1(3)-4(4)+2(-6) \\\\  & =-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-19<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-31<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434765 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475169 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434765 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475170 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434765 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475171 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434765 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475172 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434765 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164154" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434765 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434765 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/81/81604581fae8b25776727eca4450caa7d48c42a781706455ed585d5cfc4a25fa.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434765 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9cc186b-22af-55d8-bf89-1dd19ae7b90f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434765 #edb/ref 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A$ and $C.$ Hence $A$ and $C$ are local maximum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $A({x}_{A},{y}_{A},{z}_{A}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{A},{y}_{A})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $A$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](images/36/3651b43178819666002775a26071a0bd2c9cb48b58a3c5516c38e085d50fc4a6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1045 "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186445202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445191 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "632f0c8c-4357-5396-aad3-4401d3471d8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445192 #edb/ref 1080 "The function does not have local maxima" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445192 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445192 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "118339a7-669b-5081-8175-449ccce667f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445193 #edb/ref 1080 "$B$ and $C$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445193 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445193 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "80185868-d8d6-5a9a-9b8a-83d741afd936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445194 #edb/ref 1080 "$C$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445194 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445194 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b251a48a-e4be-5932-a217-f573cfba9f2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445195 #edb/ref 1080 "$B$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445195 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445195 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b9b3e9e9-2073-5961-8289-000100206e82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445196 #edb/ref 1080 "$A$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445196 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445196 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e2b33820-f35d-5434-abf3-958f348e4695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445197 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445197 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445197 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445197 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445197 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445197 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445197 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445197 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186445196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445197 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "261ceadd-9e63-5d86-9762-c5e81f0679da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445198 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445198 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445198 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445198 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/19/19f4f2d616fa648fcd4663be22b32ab18662fffa8e31f9bd44f0dd3b6da99208.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445198 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da2e1b4b-fd99-52e9-91fa-fb0d43968219" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445198 #edb/ref 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch region exists for the point $A.$ Hence, $A$ is a local maximum point.\n\n**Note**: For the point $A({x}_{A},{y}_{A},{z}_{A}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{A},{y}_{A})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $A$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](images/f0/f0b16c2cb372280ec39915b0e8d4e396e68baec9a8e20edc5389ced079648836.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445199 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186445203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445199 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40399da2-21fd-5f08-bac2-f30a1d0cf8b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445199 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186367874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445200 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186445201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445200 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "781a150f-a171-5196-8aca-b895a99d8faa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445200 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186445198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445201 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7aa4c459-af9e-558f-a889-0dc28bf20ece" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445201 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186284450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445202 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186445199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445202 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f285dabd-6305-5f61-b401-ef0b24f27f1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445202 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445203 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186445200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445203 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f301e8ab-1294-53d5-b248-897ded01ea54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445203 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186401439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186465501 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186465501 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186465501 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cb319d62-cd43-5416-b0dd-4e83919c9d00" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186465502 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186465502 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186465502 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ccb461f